Git merge combines two branches by creating a merge commit and preserves the complete history, resulting in a non-linear graph. 
Git rebase moves your commits on top of another branch, rewriting history and producing a clean, linear commit graph. 
Merge is safer for shared branches, while rebase is preferred for keeping feature branches clean.

